---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { phases } from '../data/phases.js';
---
<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">free · plain english · self-paced</p>
    <h1>Computer Science for <span class="hl">Vibe Coders</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">You can already build apps with AI. Learn what's happening underneath, so you keep the speed and lose the bugs.</p>
    <div class="term" aria-label="Example terminal">
      <div class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
<pre><span class="p">you@laptop</span>:<span class="g">~/my-app</span>$ ask-ai "why is it broken?"
<span class="c"># pasted the same error 5 times. still broken.</span>
<span class="p">you@laptop</span>:<span class="g">~/my-app</span>$ learn --cs-fundamentals
<span class="g">✓</span> mental model loaded. bugs now make sense.</pre>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/phase-1-machine">./start --phase 1</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/prompt">get the CS Coach prompt</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="tldr"><b>TL;DR</b>Use AI as a <strong>tutor, not a crutch</strong>. 4 phases + a shipping checklist. A few hours a week.</p>

  <details><summary><span class="t">The idea: from prompting to engineering</span><span class="s">Prototypes ≠ products. CS is what closes the gap.</span></summary><div class="body">
    <p>Vibe coding (describing what you want and letting AI write the code) is a genuinely great way to prototype. But a prototype and a product are different things. Real apps have to stay secure, stay fast as data grows, and keep working when you change them next month.</p>
    <p>That's what computer science and software engineering are for. You don't need a degree, and you don't need to stop using AI. You need a working mental model of the machine, the vocabulary to judge the code you're given, and a few habits that keep projects healthy.</p>
    <p>Every phase explains concepts in plain English, shows the specific mistakes AI tools tend to make, and gives you a hands-on checklist to prove you've actually got it.</p>
  </div></details>

  <h2>The four phases</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {phases.map(p => <a class="card" href={`/${p.slug}`}><span class="n">0{p.num} /</span><h3>{p.title}</h3><p>{p.short}</p></a>)}
  </div>
  <h2>Extras</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <a class="card" href="/shipping"><span class="n">bonus /</span><h3>Shipping real apps</h3><p>Git, PRs, CI, secrets, auth, migrations, RLS, tests, deploys.</p></a>
    <a class="card" href="/rules"><span class="n">habits /</span><h3>Rules of engagement</h3><p>Four anti-vibe-coding rules that build real skill.</p></a>
    <a class="card" href="/prompt"><span class="n">tool /</span><h3>The CS Coach prompt</h3><p>Turn any AI into a patient CS tutor. One-click copy.</p></a>
  </div>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Set up your tutor.</strong> Paste the <a href="/prompt">CS Coach prompt</a> into Claude, ChatGPT, Grok or your coding tool.</li>
    <li><strong>Go phase by phase.</strong> Skim the TL;DRs, expand what's new, tick the checklist.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply it to your app.</strong> Hunt for each phase's "AI mistakes" in something you built.</li>
    <li><strong>Ship carefully.</strong> Run the <a href="/shipping">shipping checklist</a> before real users touch it.</li>
  </ol>

  <details><summary><span class="t">Who it's for &amp; how long it takes</span><span class="s">Anyone who's built with AI. Phase 1 ≈ 4–6 weeks; the whole path ≈ a few months.</span></summary><div class="body">
    <p>Founders, designers, marketers, realtors, students, anyone who has built something with AI and wants to understand it well enough to trust it. If you've ever pasted an error back into a chat five times in a row, this is for you.</p>
    <p>Estimated pace: a few hours a week. Phase 1 takes about 4–6 weeks alongside CS50x; the whole path takes a few months. Go at your own speed.</p>
  </div></details>
</Base>
